(ns envelope.seal-sync
  "Synchronous ClojureScript byte backend for the same envelope wire format as
  `envelope.seal` (Web Crypto, Promises) and `envelope.seal-jvm` (JCA).

  It exists for callers that are synchronous by construction and run on a
  Node-compatible host without a JVM: kbb/nbb command-line clients (a Git
  remote helper, `bonsai.private-repo`), where every function answering a
  Promise would turn a synchronous API into an asynchronous one. Its surface
  is `envelope.seal-jvm`'s, function for function, so a `.cljk` caller can
  require `#?(:clj envelope.seal-jvm :cljs envelope.seal-sync)` and change
  nothing else. It adds what `seal-jvm` does not have: the hybrid
  `:x25519+ml-kem-768` wraps, opened and created.

  ## Where each primitive comes from, and why

  - **X25519: `kotoba-lang/org-signal`** (`kotoba.signal.x25519`, audited
    @noble/curves), the same call `envelope.seal` makes. This repo's rule is
    that a second X25519 in the workspace is a second one to get wrong, so
    `org-ietf-x25519` -- portable, but by its own docstring not
    constant-time -- is not used here.
  - **ML-KEM-768: `envelope.kem`**, i.e. the one @noble/post-quantum binding
    `envelope.qualify` measures for `evaluate-pq-provider`. The hybrid
    transcript is `kem/combiner-input`, the same definition the async
    backend hashes, so the two cannot drift.
  - **AES-256-GCM, HMAC-SHA256 (under HKDF) and randomness: `node:crypto`**
    (OpenSSL).
    org-signal's ClojureScript HKDF is Web Crypto and answers a Promise, and
    Web Crypto has no synchronous AES-GCM. On Node, Web Crypto IS this
    OpenSSL, so the async backend and this one compute AES and HKDF with the
    same provider through different doors. The portable alternatives were
    rejected on the rules this workspace already wrote down:
    `kotoba-lang/security` keeps its own AEAD/HKDF platform-native
    (\"no hand-rolled cipher/MAC\"), its crypto inventory names AES-256-GCM
    by a platform provider (`qualification/crypto-policy.edn`, `:jca`), and
    `org-nist-aes` says of itself that a table-lookup AES is not
    constant-time and not for a process whose cache an attacker can
    measure. A synchronous HKDF belongs in org-signal; until it has one, the
    host's is used rather than a third implementation written here.
  - **Randomness** is `node:crypto` `randomBytes` for content keys and IVs,
    and `crypto.getRandomValues` (inside @noble) for X25519 and ML-KEM keys:
    a CSPRNG everywhere, never a PRNG.

  Bytes are Uint8Array in and out, as in `envelope.seal`. Failures throw
  (synchronously) where `envelope.seal` rejects: a tampered ciphertext, a
  wrong key, a wrong AAD, a hybrid entry without its ML-KEM key, a classical
  wrap presented as a hybrid one or the reverse. Which construction opens an
  entry is read off the entry, never negotiated."
  (:require [
                       [envelope.model :as m]
                       [envelope.kem :as kem]
                       [kotoba.signal.x25519 :as x25519]]))


